How many questions come from the p-block in JEE Advanced every year?

On average, JEE Advanced asks 4-6 questions from p-block elements across both papers. The exact count varies by year, but p-block is among the top 3 most tested inorganic chapters, alongside coordination compounds and chemical bonding.

Which p-block topics are most important for JEE Advanced?

Phosphorus compounds (allotropes, oxoacids, reactions with NaOH), halogen oxoacids (HClO to HClO₄ structure and acidity), and nitrogen oxides (structures, N-N bonds, oxidation states) are the highest-priority topics based on frequency of appearance from 2009 to 2023.

Is NCERT enough for the p-block in JEE Advanced?

NCERT is necessary but not sufficient. JEE Advanced asks application-based and multi-concept questions that go beyond NCERT's factual content. You need NCERT for the foundation and then must practise JEE-level questions like those compiled on this page.

How do I approach paragraph-based questions in JEE Advanced p-block?

Read the paragraph once to identify the chain of reagents. Write the oxidation state of the key element at each step. Confirm the product at each stage before moving to the next. Do not assume - verify each transformation. In the 2013 and 2017 paragraphs above, missing the first step (identifying R) would have made all subsequent answers wrong.

What is a disproportionation reaction, and why does it appear so often in p-block questions?

disproportionation reaction is one where a single element simultaneously undergoes both oxidation and reduction. It appears frequently in p-block because elements like phosphorus, chlorine, and nitrogen have multiple stable oxidation states, making disproportionation chemically feasible and conceptually testable. Leave a comment
