Find answers to common questions.

How do you identify positive vs. negative deviation from Raoult's law?

Positive deviation occurs when A-B intermolecular forces in the mixture are weaker than A-A or B-B forces in pure liquids (e.g., CCl₄ + methanol, CS₂ + acetone). Vapour pressure increases above the Raoult's law line. Negative deviation occurs when A-B forces are stronger than in pure components (e.g., phenol + aniline, chloroform + acetone). Vapour pressure falls below the ideal line.

What is the van't Hoff factor and why does it matter in JEE Advanced?

The van't Hoff factor (i) corrects colligative property formulas for solutes that dissociate (i > 1) or associate (i < 1) in solution. JEE Advanced routinely hides i inside questions about coordination compounds or dimerizing solutes. Calculating i incorrectly is the single most common error in this chapter. Always check whether the solute is an electrolyte, a complex, or an associating molecule before writing any formula.

Is Liquid Solution important for JEE Advanced?

iquid Solution consistently appears in JEE Advanced every year. From 2009 to 2018, at least one question per paper came from this chapter, and many years had two or three questions. Colligative properties and Raoult's law are the most frequently tested subtopics. Given the relatively contained syllabus, this chapter offers a high return on study time.

How is eSaral's approach to Physical Chemistry different from other platforms?

eSaral's Physical Chemistry is taught by IIT Bombay faculty, including AIR-41 rankers, who teach the same Kota-quality problem-solving methods online at a fraction of the cost. The 5-layer doubt solving system ensures no question stays unresolved, and the structured PYQ practice with detailed solution videos mirrors the approach used by JEE toppers who consistently clear the Advanced cutoff

Which topics in Liquid Solution are asked most in JEE Advanced?

Based on the recent data: colligative properties (boiling point elevation, freezing point depression) appear in ~60% of years, Raoult's law deviations in ~40%, Henry's law in ~20%, and thermodynamics of mixing in ~10%. Prioritise colligative properties and Raoult's law graph interpretation for maximum marks impact.

What is Henry's law and how is it applied in JEE Advanced questions?

Henry's law states that the partial pressure of a gas above a solution equals the Henry's law constant multiplied by the mole fraction of the gas dissolved: P = K_H × x. In JEE Advanced, always calculate the partial pressure of the gas using Dalton's law (P_gas = mole fraction in gas phase × total pressure) before applying Henry's law. The mole fraction in the gas phase and in solution are different quantities. Leave a comment
